ontSize;\n    36\t}\n    37\t\n    38\t/**\n    39\t * The text style of tick labels, read from the text layer's own computed style, so measurement agrees with\n    40\t * what the layer renders (it uses `font-size: max(12px, 1em)`), including the page's text spacing (spec A6).\n    41\t */\n    42\texport function readTextStyle(element: Element): TextStyle {\n    43\t  const win = element.ownerDocument.defaultView;\n    44\t  const style = win?.getComputedStyle(element);\n    45\t  const fontSize = style ? parseFloat(style.fontSize) || 12 : 12;\n    46\t  if (!style) return { font: `${fontSize}px sans-serif`, letterSpacing: 0, wordSpacing: 0, lineHeight: fontSize * 1.2 };\n    47\t  const family = style.fontFamily || 'sans-serif';\n    48\t  const font = `${style.fontStyle || 'normal'} ${style.fontWeight || '400'} ${fontSize}px ${family}`;\n    49\t  const letterSpacing = pixels(style.letterSpacing, fontSize);\n    50\t  const wordSpacing = pixels(style.wordSpacing, fontSize);\n    51\t  const lineHeight = pixels(style.lineHeight, fontSize);\n    52\t  return {\n    53\t    font,\n    54\t    letterSpacing: Number.isFinite(letterSpacing) ? letterSpacing : 0,\n    55\t    wordSpacing: Number.isFinite(wordSpacing) ? wordSpacing : 0,\n    56\t    // 'normal' depends on the font's metrics; 1.2 is the common value and errs on the side of room.\n    57\t    lineHeight: Number.isFinite(lineHeight) ? lineHeight : Math.ceil(fontSize * 1.2),\n    58\t  };\n    59\t}\n    60\t\n    61\tconst contexts = new WeakMap<Document, CanvasRenderingContext2D | null>();\n    62\t/** Measured widths per document, for every style together, so the bound holds for the whole page. */\n    63\tconst caches = new WeakMap<Document, Map<string, number>>();\n    64\tconst fontEpochs = new WeakMap<Document, number>();\n    65\texport const MEASUREMENT_CACHE_LIMIT = 8192;\n    66\t\n    67\t/**\n    68\t * Web fonts finished loading in `doc`: widths measured with a fallback font are wrong now. Every chart of\n    69\t * the document calls this on the same event; the first clears the cache and the epoch moves on.\n    70\t */\n    71\texport function noteFontLoad(doc: Document): void {\n    72\t  fontEpochs.set(doc, (fontEpochs.get(doc) ?? 0) + 1);\n    73\t  caches.get(doc)?.clear();\n    74\t}\n    75\t\n    76\t/** How many widths the document's cache holds (for tests of its bound). */\n    77\texport function measurementCacheSize(doc: Document): number {\n    78\t  return caches.get(doc)?.size ?? 0;\n    79\t}\n    80\t\n    81\ttype SpacingContext = CanvasRenderingContext2D & { letterSpacing?: string; wordSpacing?: string };\n    82\t\n    83\t/**\n    84\t * Canvas `measureText` with the computed font and the page's letter and word spacing (spec A6). Where the\n    85\t * context supports `letterSpacing` and `wordSpacing` they are set on it, so the browser adds them as it\n    86\t * does for the text layer; otherwise they are added the way CSS adds them (after every character, and\n    87\t * after every space). Cached per document, font epoch, style and string.\n    88\t */\n    89\texport function createMeasurer(doc: Document): TextMeasurer {\n    90\t  return {\n    91\t    width(text: string, style: TextStyle): number {\n    92\t      let ctx = contexts.get(doc);\n    93\t      if (ctx === undefined) {\n    94\t        ctx = doc.createElement('canvas').getContext('2d');\n    95\t        contexts.set(doc, ctx);\n    96\t      }\n    97\t      let cache = caches.get(doc);\n    98\t      if (!cache) caches.set(doc, cache = new Map());\n    99\t      const key = `${fontEpochs.get(doc) ?? 0}|${style.font}|${style.letterSpacing}|${style.wordSpacing}|${text}`;\n   100\t      const hit = cache.get(key);\n   101\t      if (hit !== undefined) return hit;\n   102\t      let width: number;\n   103\t      let spaced = false;\n   104\t      if (ctx) {\n   105\t        const c = ctx as SpacingContext;\n   106\t        if (c.font !== style.font) c.font = style.font;\n   107\t        if (typeof c.letterSpacing === 'string' && typeof c.wordSpacing === 'string') {\n   108\t          const letter = `${style.letterSpacing}px`;\n   109\t          const word = `${style.wordSpacing}px`;\n   110\t          if (c.letterSpacing !== letter) c.letterSpacing = letter;\n   111\t          if (c.wordSpacing !== word) c.wordSpacing = word;\n   112\t          spaced = true;\n   113\t        }\n   114\t        width = c.measureText(text).width;\n   115\t      } else {\n   116\t        width = text.length * parseFloat(/(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?)px/.exec(style.font)?.[1] ?? '12') * 0.6;\n   117\t      }\n   118\t      if (!spaced) {\n   119\t        if (style.letterSpacing !== 0) width += style.letterSpacing * [...text].length;\n   120\t        if (style.wordSpacing !== 0) {\n   121\t          let spaces = 0;\n   122\t          for (let i = 0; i < text.length; i++) if (text.charCodeAt(i) === 32) spaces++;\n   123\t          width += style.wordSpacing * spaces;\n   124\t        }\n   125\t      }\n   126\t      if (cache.size >= MEASUREMENT_CACHE_LIMIT) cache.clear();\n   127\t      cache.set(key, width);\n   128\t      return width;\n   129\t    },\n   130\t  };\n   131\t}\n   132\t\n   133\texport function sameTextStyle(a: TextStyle | null, b: TextStyle): boolean {\n   134\t  return a !== null && a.font === b.font && a.letterSpacing === b.letterSpacing &&\n   135\t    a.wordSpacing === b.wordSpacing && a.lineHeight === b.lineHeight;\n   136\t}\n   137\t\n   138\t/**\n   139\t * Calls `onChange` when the device pixel ratio changes (browser zoom, moving to another screen), when\n   140\t * reduced motion is toggled, and when web fonts finish loading. Returns `stop`.\n   141\t */\n   142\texport function watchEnvironment(win: Window, handlers: { pixelRatio(): void; motion(): void; fonts(): void }): () => void {\n   143\t  let stopped = false;\n   144\t  // Each subscription registers its removal as soon as it exists, so a failure part-way (a matchMedia that\n   145\t  // throws) leaves none behind (review 3, finding 9).\n   146\t  const undo: (() => void)[] = [];\n   147\t  const stop = (): void => {\n   148\t    stopped = true;\n   149\t    for (let fn = undo.pop(); fn; fn = undo.pop()) {\n   150\t      try { fn(); } catch { /* keep releasing */ }\n   151\t    }\n   152\t  };\n   153\t  try {\n   154\t    let ratioQuery: MediaQueryList | null = null;\n   155\t    const onRatio = (): void => {\n   156\t      if (stopped) return;\n   157\t      arm();\n   158\t      handlers.pixelRatio();\n   159\t    };\n   160\t    // A resolution query matches one ratio only, so it is re-armed for the new ratio after each change.\n   161\t    const arm = (): void => {\n   162\t      ratioQuery?.removeEventListener('change', onRatio);\n   163\t      ratioQuery = win.matchMedia(`(resolution: ${win.devicePixelRatio}dppx)`);\n   164\t      ratioQuery.addEventListener('change', onRatio);\n   165\t    };\n   166\t    arm();\n   167\t    undo.push(() => ratioQuery?.removeEventListener('change', onRatio));\n   168\t    const motion = win.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');\n   169\t    const onMotion = (): void => { if (!stopped) handlers.motion(); };\n   170\t    motion.addEventListener('change', onMotion);\n   171\t    undo.push(() => motion.removeEventListener('change', onMotion));\n   172\t    const fonts = win.document.fonts as FontFaceSet | undefined;\n   173\t    const onFonts = (): void => { if (!stopped) handlers.fonts(); };\n   174\t    fonts?.addEventListener('loadingdone', onFonts);\n   175\t    undo.push(() => fonts?.removeEventListener('loadingdone', onFonts));\n   176\t  } catch (error) {\n   177\t    stop();\n   178\t    throw error;\n   179\t  }\n   180\t  return stop;\n   181\t}\n   182\t\n   183\t/**\n   184\t * Calls `onChange` when a `lang` attribute changes on `element` or an ancestor: the page's language decides\n   185\t * the formatting locale and which built-in text is marked English (spec A10, F6). An environment change,\n   186\t * kept apart from the theme watcher. Returns `stop`.\n   187\t */\n   188\texport function watchLanguage(element: Element, onChange: () => void): () => void {\n   189\t  const win = element.ownerDocument.defaultView;\n   190\t  if (win === null) return () => {};\n   191\t  const observer = new win.MutationObserver(records => {\n   192\t    for (const r of records) {\n   193\t      if (r.target instanceof win.Node && r.target.contains(element)) { onChange(); return; }\n   194\t    }\n   195\t  });\n   196\t  observer.observe(element.ownerDocument.documentElement, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['lang'], subtree: true });\n   197\t  return () => observer.disconnect();\n   198\t}\n9:Changed later the same day, in the owner's words: \"try to use more antropic model than openai , use openai model for another providier review\". From that point: building and fixing, in every module including those Codex wrote (data, layout, simplify, view, scene, lookup, reader, messages, diagnostics, the Node acceptance suite, the acceptance matrix and checker), goes to Anthropic models (Opus 5.5 high for code; Opus 5.5 xhigh or Fable 5.2 for harder reasoning). Codex models are used read-only, as the other-provider reviewer of that work. Work done before the change keeps its recorded authors.\n17:| M0 Foundations | Done: workspace, toolchain, contracts, benchmark harness (first version), build and size scripts. Not done: the acceptance matrix as data, CI workflows (no remote exists) |\n22:| M5 Bar, area, donut, compact, scatter | All render through the shared pipeline. Type-specific acceptance tests, the palette, and donut legend: in progress or not started |\n24:| M7 Distribution and documentation | Not started: React adapter, documentation site, packed-distribution tests, size check against targets |\n35:| Controller: final review findings (three blockers in the displayed-snapshot design, paused validation, validate parity, cached-layout findings, temporal snapshots, compact zoom, watcher cleanup), readout mode as floating / strip / none, benchmark hooks, the 24-chart resize mismatch (agent a1d73b9f46c46814f, resumed) | Claude Opus 5.5 high | `src/core` except `focus.ts`, `src/kinds`, `src/theme/watch.ts`, `test/core` |\n36:| Interaction layer: decision 0004 revision 3 as one state machine; the final review's 16 findings, 16 earlier partials, 10 mutations (agent aa0e6fca8e4a6224b, new) | Claude Opus 5.5 high | `src/dom`, `src/core/focus.ts`, figure section of `theme/css.ts`, `test/browser/chart`, `test/browser/a11y` |\n38:| Engine switch in the browser harness; the acceptance suite in Playwright Firefox 159, then WebKit 27.2 (agent a61d95e46b3f0f3aa, resumed) | Claude Opus 5.5 high | `test/browser/acceptance`, `harness.ts`, the two shared util files |\n40:Final reviews, both \"do not release yet\": controller, Codex gpt-6.2-sol max, `wp/core-final.controller.review.md` (5 blocking, 11 major; the restructuring itself found clean); interaction layer, Codex gpt-6.2-astra xhigh, `wp/core-final.interaction.review.md` (5 blocking, 11 major). Decision 0004 is at revision 3 (one rest-and-corridor rule; a hold never changes what a click activates; three readout states; controls and focus rings never covered). Spec clarified: F7's first-tick rule is for value axes; F9 asks 32 distinct cue combinations for every chart type; I1 covers plots too short for the strip.\n42:Owner decisions on 2026-11-18: palette candidate B adopted (9861f54); size targets 96 KiB everything and 80 KiB line only (spec DX7); licence stays MIT with holder \"The Axylo authors\"; Playwright browser downloads allowed (Firefox and WebKit installed under ~/.cache/ms-playwright; the owner installed WebKit's system libraries); no screen-reader rehearsal for 1.0 (state it as not done, do not block on it); the React adapter is removed from 1.0 (bf93e1a; last state at dac5664; adapters for React, Vue, Svelte later; plain TypeScript for now).\n44:State at a518cbb (coordinator runs; not a recorded run): type check clean; Node suite 1 known failure (the kept P7 memory test); core browser suites 834 pass, 1 skipped, on the restructured build with palette B (run before the React removal, which does not touch them); distribution 83 Node and 26 browser pass; docs 218 unit pass (160 browser per the author); benchmark harness 97 pure tests pass. Sizes from the packed files: everything 104.24 KiB (target 96), line only 87.50 (target 80), donut only 75.32, validate only 20.40.\n46:Committed since the last checkpoint: per-chart composition and the cheap size cuts (37ed261); palette B and its independent check; docs fix pass after review and its maintenance; distribution maintenance; benchmark harness third pass (instrumented and release builds apart; `--gpu`; a second decision family for GPU workloads); React removal across packages, distribution tests, docs and dev dependencies.\n48:For the core's author after the final reviews (one pass): review findings; the benchmark hooks in `src/core/instrument.ts` (`series[i].breaks`, `describe().plot`, `memory()` returning `{ bytes, buffers }`, `series[i].keptBasis === 'window'` for streams); the unexplained 24-chart resize mismatch (marks up to 13 px from the reported domain); library oddities from the docs author (`wp/docs.fix.report.md`: a wide-row summary uses the field key and puts the unit on the minimum only; the hidden plot control reads \"No data\" in the too-small state; AX199 wording); a size pass toward 96 and 80 KiB; the deferred P7 and scatter-pick items. Small record fixes for the palette author: `wp/palette-b.review.md` findings 1 to 6 (decision line 14 still says nobody else reviewed B).\n55:- Not started: documentation site, packed-distribution tests, size check against targets, scatter renderer measurement, a dist build for `@axylo/react` (must define `__DEV__` and keep the `use client` banner).\n61:2. A short Codex verification of the fixes against the two final reviews (read-only), then record every configuration on a quiet tree, Firefox and WebKit included; the matrix must be re-merged by an Anthropic model now (engines; React rows gone; DX1, DX7, DX8 amended; A3 rehearsal recorded as not done by the owner's decision) and reviewed by Codex.\n62:3. A size pass toward 96 and 80 KiB (currently 104.24 and 87.50).\n64:5. Conformance table (A9) from the acceptance evidence, stating no screen reader was run; agent rehearsal (DX12) against the docs; Phase 1 comparison rerun with Axylo included; docs follow-up for decision 0004 revision 3 and changed diagnostics wording.\n70:- **GPU rendering can be tested here.** Headless Chrome uses software rendering by default, which is all the earlier runs used. With `--enable-gpu --use-angle=gl --ignore-gpu-blocklist`, or run headed on the desktop, Chrome reports the NVIDIA GPU. The benchmark's GPU-dependent workloads (resize, pixel ratio 2, process memory) are therefore open work, not a limit.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
