{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git diff a518cbb bbd7564 -- packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts docs/02-product-spec.md; git show bbd7564:packages/axylo/src/scale/nice.ts | nl -ba | sed -n '1,190p'; git show bbd7564:packages/axylo/src/layout/labels.ts | rg -n 'function fitBottom|function fitContinuum|function continuous|partial'\"","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/docs/02-product-spec.md b/docs/02-product-spec.md\nindex 8e23912..71829f9 100644\n--- a/docs/02-product-spec.md\n+++ b/docs/02-product-spec.md\n@@ -97,9 +97,9 @@ Every criterion maps to at least one test in the acceptance matrix kept in the r\n | F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\n | F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n | F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n-| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. The automatic domain contains all data, the reference lines and bands the chart draws (a compact chart draws none, so `reference` has no effect there and is reported as an option that does not apply) and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n+| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. This rule is for value axes: the y axis of line, area and bar, and both axes of scatter. A continuous x axis of line, area or bar spans exactly its data (plus half a band for bars), so a time series is not padded to the next tick. The automatic domain contains all data, the reference lines and bands the chart draws (a compact chart draws none, so `reference` has no effect there and is reported as an option that does not apply) and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n | F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices that have an arc (a positive value), the seven largest are drawn and the rest of those are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; zero and `null` slices never count toward the limit and are never grouped, so each stays listed on its own; the table lists every category. Shares are shown with at most one fraction digit. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\n-| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars: color and fill pattern, which gives 32 distinct combinations; beyond 32 bar series combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n+| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars, full and compact alike: color and fill pattern; for every chart type at least 32 distinct combinations, after which combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n | F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\n | F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\n | F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\n@@ -121,7 +121,7 @@ Every criterion maps to at least one test in the acceptance matrix kept in the r\n \n | ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n |---|---|---|---|\n-| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark or any control, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". In a plot too small for it (decision 0004: narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px) the same content appears as text in a strip reserved under the plot. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\n+| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark or any control, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". In a plot too small for it (decision 0004: narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px) the same content appears as text in a strip reserved under the plot; where even the strip would leave the plot under the minimum of L8, pointing highlights the datum without a readout and the values are in the keyboard reading and the table. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\n | I2 | Legend entries are toggle buttons with a pressed state, operable by click, Enter and Space. | Keyboard and accessibility-tree tests | R12 |\n | I3 | Zoom on time, date and numeric x axes is reachable four ways: visible Zoom in, Zoom out and Reset buttons that work with single clicks; drag to select a range; `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows; and pinch when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. The wheel zooms only while the plot has focus, and never when Ctrl or Meta is held, so browser zoom and page scrolling are not intercepted. | Reach a given range by clicks only; by keyboard only; by drag; page scrolls normally with the pointer over an unfocused chart | D7, R12, WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7, 1.4.4 |\n | I4 | Every interaction works with a single pointer without dragging, and by touch. Every control is at least 24×24 CSS px. A one-finger drag on a chart scrolls the page. | Touch emulation; size checks | WCAG 2.5.7, 2.5.8 |\ndiff --git a/packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts b/packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts\nindex 63b3f04..3349437 100644\n--- a/packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts\n+++ b/packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts\n@@ -125,6 +125,8 @@ export interface PointSeries {\n    * and do not appear here; a value that cannot be plotted is never imputed.\n    */\n   readonly missingX?: Float64Array;\n+  /** Source row index of each entry of `missingX`, in the same order; present whenever `missingX` and `row` are. */\n+  readonly missingRow?: Int32Array;\n }\n \n /**\n@@ -630,7 +632,9 @@ export interface Lookup {\n   /**\n    * What is under a pixel of the plot container, or null. Scatter: among the points within 24 CSS px of the\n    * pointer, the closest; points whose distance differs from the closest one's by less than half a device\n-   * pixel are tied with it, and a tie goes to the latest source row. A donut slice too thin to see is drawn,\n+   * pixel are tied with it, and a tie goes to the latest source row. To keep a pick's cost independent of the\n+   * number of points, positions may be compared after rounding to the device-pixel lattice (an error of at most\n+   * 0.71 device px); the datum's reported values are always exact. A donut slice too thin to see is drawn,\n    * and picked, at a minimum of 1 CSS px of arc; its value and share are reported unchanged.\n    */\n   pick(px: number, py: number): PickTarget | null;\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * Nice steps: 1, 2 or 5 times a power of ten. Multiples of a step are computed from integers so that\n     3\t * 3 × 0.1 comes out as 0.3, not 0.30000000000000004, and ticks compare exactly with domain ends.\n     4\t */\n     5\t\n     6\texport interface NiceStep {\n     7\t  /** 1, 2 or 5. */\n     8\t  readonly mantissa: 1 | 2 | 5;\n     9\t  readonly exponent: number;\n    10\t  /** mantissa × 10^exponent as a number. */\n    11\t  readonly value: number;\n    12\t}\n    13\t\n    14\tfunction make(mantissa: 1 | 2 | 5, exponent: number): NiceStep {\n    15\t  return { mantissa, exponent, value: multiple(1, mantissa, exponent) };\n    16\t}\n    17\t\n    18\tfunction multiple(i: number, mantissa: number, exponent: number): number {\n    19\t  const n = i * mantissa;\n    20\t  // Dividing an exact integer by an exact power of ten gives the double nearest the decimal value.\n    21\t  if (exponent >= 0) return n * 10 ** exponent;\n    22\t  if (exponent >= -300) return n / 10 ** -exponent;\n    23\t  return n * 10 ** exponent;\n    24\t}\n    25\t\n    26\t/** The smallest nice step at or above `x` (x > 0 and finite). */\n    27\texport function niceStepAtLeast(x: number): NiceStep {\n    28\t  const exponent = Math.floor(Math.log10(x));\n    29\t  const base = 10 ** exponent;\n    30\t  const r = x / base;\n    31\t  // Tolerance: log10 and the division are off by an ulp or two for values like 0.2 or 5e-7.\n    32\t  const tol = 1 + 1e-9;\n    33\t  if (r <= tol) return make(1, exponent);\n    34\t  if (r <= 2 * tol) return make(2, exponent);\n    35\t  if (r <= 5 * tol) return make(5, exponent);\n    36\t  return make(1, exponent + 1);\n    37\t}\n    38\t\n    39\t/** The next coarser nice step. */\n    40\texport function nextNiceStep(s: NiceStep): NiceStep {\n    41\t  if (s.mantissa === 1) return make(2, s.exponent);\n    42\t  if (s.mantissa === 2) return make(5, s.exponent);\n    43\t  return make(1, s.exponent + 1);\n    44\t}\n    45\t\n    46\t/** i × step, cleaned. */\n    47\texport function stepMultiple(i: number, s: NiceStep): number {\n    48\t  const v = multiple(i, s.mantissa, s.exponent);\n    49\t  return v === 0 ? 0 : v; // no -0\n    50\t}\n    51\t\n    52\t/**\n    53\t * Index of the first multiple of the step at or above x. The quotient is off by at most an index or two\n    54\t * from rounding, so the estimate starts at the plain quotient and is corrected by comparing the actual\n    55\t * multiples with x. (A relative tolerance on the quotient would move a large index by dozens of steps.)\n    56\t */\n    57\texport function firstIndexAtOrAbove(x: number, s: NiceStep): number {\n    58\t  let i = Math.ceil(x / s.value);\n    59\t  // Bounded: where the step is below the resolution of x, neighbouring multiples are the same double.\n    60\t  for (let n = 0; n < 8 && stepMultiple(i, s) < x; n++) i++;\n    61\t  for (let n = 0; n < 8 && stepMultiple(i - 1, s) >= x; n++) i--;\n    62\t  return i;\n    63\t}\n    64\t\n    65\t/** Index of the last multiple of the step at or below x. */\n    66\texport function lastIndexAtOrBelow(x: number, s: NiceStep): number {\n    67\t  let i = Math.floor(x / s.value);\n    68\t  for (let n = 0; n < 8 && stepMultiple(i, s) > x; n++) i--;\n    69\t  for (let n = 0; n < 8 && stepMultiple(i + 1, s) <= x; n++) i++;\n    70\t  return i;\n    71\t}\n    72\t\n    73\t/** Most ticks in the densest candidate set. Layout never fits more on a readable axis. */\n    74\texport const MAX_TICKS = 12;\n22:  readonly partial?: boolean;\n353:export function fitBottom(\n465:  return { ...result(labels.filter(label => kept.has(label)), side, candidates.length > 1 ? ['thinned'] : []), partial: true };\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"agent_message","text":"The Node probes reproduced two release-blocking cases. A hidden, paused chart retained 502 rows despite `keep: 1`, and a typed-column append returned the opening row when hovering a newly appended point. I’m also checking which existing tests miss those cases."}}
