er, chart, stages, delivery, environment, axes, options, composition, instrument), `packages/axylo/src/kinds/**`, `packages/axylo/src/index.ts`, and the per-shape entry points the compositions call. Requirements are unchanged: `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md` sections 1.5 and 2; `docs/02-product-spec.md` (F1–F12, DX2–DX8, P7, registry 3.5, type matrix 3.6, compact 3.7; several sentences were amended on 2026-11-18: read F7, F8, F9, F10, I1, L8, DX1, DX7, DX8 and the zoom row as they are now); `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `public.ts`; decisions in `docs/decisions/` (0004 revision 2 with rule 3b).\n     8\t\n     9\tThis is the last review before the acceptance evidence is recorded, so spend the effort where a defect would reach a user:\n    10\t\n    11\t1. **Your 20 earlier findings**: for each, fixed, partly, not fixed, or fixed with a new defect. Read the code path and rerun your original sequence in Node where you had one; do not take a report's or a test's word. The author says these are not done or only partly: finding 11 (opening a store on a large static chart still holds about 24.8 MB beyond the caller's 16 MB; a kept failing test records it) and the scatter pick bound.\n    12\t2. **The displayed-snapshot design** (comments on `release()`, `showing()`, `frame()` in the controller, and `test/core/snapshot.test.ts` with its fast-check property over random call sequences): state the invariants the code actually maintains, find a sequence that breaks one (update, append with and without keep, pause, resume, zoom, frames that run, frames that never run, frames that fail in each stage, re-entrant calls from callbacks, destroy at each point, a re-encode while paused, a replace while a frame is pending), and say what the property test's generator cannot reach.\n    13\t3. **The restructuring**: it was meant to change no behaviour. Compare each composition's pieces with what the single code path did before (`git show 08b469b:<path>` gives the pre-restructuring files): for each of the five kinds and the compact variants, is anything missing from a kind's composition that the old dispatch reached (a mark handler for overlay or focus marks, a reader path, a diagnostic emitted in a different order or not at all, a step function, a swatch), and does any shared code still assume all kinds are loaded? The diagnostic registry became data with messages composed in `createDiagnostic`: diff every code's message, level, effect and fix text against the old registry for at least the inputs in the registry tests, and say if anything changed. `validate(type, options)` must still give the same diagnostics as a created chart for every kind.\n    14\t4. **Hooks and build constants**: `__DEV__` and `__BENCH__`, the guard expression, what a production bundle can still do that it should not (or cannot do that it should: AX199 recovery, `strict`, diagnostics delivery), and whether any behaviour differs between development and production beyond console output of hints.\n    15\t5. **Anything new** in the code paths added since your last review that you have not seen: events delivering x in the form `zoom()` accepts; category and donut zoom as a reported no-op; option snapshots on acceptance; AX160 buckets; readout mode decided by layout; the language watcher; `missingX`; compact charts ignoring `reference` and tick formats; diagnostic naming from the target's id.\n    16\t\n    17\tFor each finding: severity (B blocks a release, M major, m minor), file and line, the concrete call sequence, what goes wrong, which requirement it violates, and the fix. At most 16 findings, most severe first; leave out style. No praise. End with the table for part 1, one line per area you examined closely and found sound, and a plain statement: would you release this controller, and if not, what exactly stands in the way.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
